{
 "step1_causal_chain": "A device history is reusable only while its blocks are still in a cache tier (FORMULATION.md: the physical component of context inertia, where reconstruction cost grows with context length). Under write-through every block computed on GPU is also stored on host, so the only thing that removes a history is eviction, and eviction is least-recency-first on each tier independently. Therefore the tokens written to each tier since a history's last refresh, together with the refresh rule (which StudyENG1c Part A identified), decide whether the next request for that history is served from GPU, from host, or recomputed. All of those inputs are things a scheduler knows when it issues the requests, so the claim under test is that bookkeeping alone predicts the tier.",
